[{:db/id 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7ccf7d0b-49a0-5735-9f73-94b4693e10fe"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ccf7d0b-49a0-5735-9f73-94b4693e10fe" :question/id #uuid "7ccf7d0b-49a0-5735-9f73-94b4693e10fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-125740" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,-1,1)$ to $(2,1,0)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,-1,1)$ and $B(2,1,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,1,0)-f(0,-1,1) \\\\  & =(2(1)^{2}+{0}^{3})-(0(-1)^{2}+{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ee6eda1f-7c7b-5e84-ad86-9a336560bf16"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ee6eda1f-7c7b-5e84-ad86-9a336560bf16" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6eda1f-7c7b-5e84-ad86-9a336560bf16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c6a59df4-b60e-57d1-8bb8-0ef8a574c18d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c6a59df4-b60e-57d1-8bb8-0ef8a574c18d" "answer-d0ea3063-2fe4-5c0e-9794-3156030da63d" "answer-da2b77e0-70de-507a-844b-baad5473a302" "answer-4e2f20f0-48bb-505a-a0bb-3e4578a2d1ff" "an